1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,954
n8n is an automation tool.

2
00:00:01,954 --> 00:00:06,950
You have probably used something in its family without knowing the name: a system

3
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:11,577
that watches for a trigger, like a new email, then runs a chain of steps in

4
00:00:11,577 --> 00:00:14,538
response. n8n's version of that chain is visual.

5
00:00:14,538 --> 00:00:19,226
You will spend this course looking at boxes and arrows, never at a terminal.

6
00:00:19,226 --> 00:00:22,804
It is open source, which matters for one practical reason.

7
00:00:22,804 --> 00:00:27,924
You can run it on your own machine for nothing, for as long as you like, and nobody

8
00:00:27,924 --> 00:00:31,378
can take it away from you or change the price on a whim.

9
00:00:31,378 --> 00:00:36,251
One honesty note. n8n also sells a hosted version, where their servers run your

10
00:00:36,251 --> 00:00:39,336
workflows so your laptop does not have to stay on.

11
00:00:39,336 --> 00:00:42,297
It costs money and this course does not need it.

12
00:00:42,297 --> 00:00:47,417
We run everything on the free, run it yourself route, and Section 2 names the exact

13
00:00:47,417 --> 00:00:51,858
moment the paid route starts earning its fee, so the choice stays yours.

14
00:00:52,408 --> 00:00:57,398
Here is what a chain actually looks like in practice, and why none of it needs code.

15
00:00:57,398 --> 00:00:58,408
An email arrives.

16
00:00:58,408 --> 00:00:59,359
One box notices.

17
00:00:59,359 --> 00:01:02,864
The next box reads it and decides what kind of email it is.

18
00:01:02,864 --> 00:01:07,794
A third files it under the right label, and a fourth adds one line to a spreadsheet

19
00:01:07,794 --> 00:01:09,042
so nothing gets lost.

20
00:01:09,042 --> 00:01:12,963
Each box does one small job and hands its result on, like a relay.

21
00:01:12,963 --> 00:01:15,695
When you can picture that, you understand n8n.

22
00:01:15,695 --> 00:01:17,240
Everything else is detail.

23
00:01:17,790 --> 00:01:21,825
n8n on its own cannot tell an urgent client query from a newsletter.

24
00:01:21,825 --> 00:01:25,385
For that, one box in each workflow calls out to an AI model.

25
00:01:25,385 --> 00:01:29,301
Which one is your choice, and Section 2 walks through picking one.

26
00:01:29,301 --> 00:01:32,624
We will start on a free tier so today costs you nothing.

27
00:01:32,624 --> 00:01:36,658
Think of it like a stapler and a good assistant. n8n is the stapler.

28
00:01:36,658 --> 00:01:40,515
The model is the assistant reading the page before it staples it.

29
00:01:40,515 --> 00:01:45,262
You need both, and they are two different subscriptions to two different things.

30
00:01:45,262 --> 00:01:47,754
What does the thinking actually look like?

31
00:01:47,754 --> 00:01:52,560
For inbox triage, the model reads the sender, the subject and the message itself,

32
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:57,128
then judges it against rules you wrote in plain English: flag anything from a

33
00:01:57,128 --> 00:02:01,875
client, ignore anything promotional, escalate anything about money or deadlines.

34
00:02:01,875 --> 00:02:04,605
Your rules live in a paragraph, not a program.

35
00:02:04,605 --> 00:02:09,411
When the agent gets something wrong, you fix it by rewriting a sentence, the same

36
00:02:09,411 --> 00:02:11,665
way you would correct a new assistant.

37
00:02:11,665 --> 00:02:15,997
That single fact is why this course can exist for people who do not code.

38
00:02:16,547 --> 00:02:18,796
You do not need to memorise this list.

39
00:02:18,796 --> 00:02:23,354
Each artifact lands exactly when you need it, and every one is designed to be

40
00:02:23,354 --> 00:02:26,137
forwarded to a colleague without embarrassment.

41
00:02:26,137 --> 00:02:30,873
A template nobody would actually use is not worth building, so none of these are

42
00:02:30,873 --> 00:02:31,346
teasers.

43
00:02:31,346 --> 00:02:34,365
They are the real thing, cut down to one page each.

44
00:02:34,365 --> 00:02:39,101
The reason the artifacts matter more than the videos is simple: the videos teach

45
00:02:39,101 --> 00:02:40,936
you, the artifacts survive you.

46
00:02:40,936 --> 00:02:45,613
Six months from now you will not rewatch Section 7 before trusting a new agent.

47
00:02:45,613 --> 00:02:50,230
You will open the trial checklist, spend ten minutes on it, and catch the same

48
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:52,420
failure Priya catches in this course.

49
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:57,393
A colleague who never took the course can still pick up the job description template

50
00:02:57,393 --> 00:02:58,340
and use it cold.

51
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:01,063
That is the test each one had to pass to ship.

52
00:03:01,613 --> 00:03:06,480
What the course does assume: you are comfortable using web apps, and you have one

53
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:09,425
task you do every week that you would rather not.

54
00:03:09,425 --> 00:03:11,167
That is the entire entry bar.

55
00:03:11,167 --> 00:03:16,214
Each build section takes thirty to sixty minutes at your own pace, and the course is

56
00:03:16,214 --> 00:03:21,262
front loaded on purpose: you build first, in Sections 3 to 6, and earn the judgement

57
00:03:21,262 --> 00:03:26,309
that keeps those builds safe in Sections 7 to 10, against mistakes you have actually

58
00:03:26,309 --> 00:03:27,090
seen by then.

59
00:03:27,090 --> 00:03:27,450
Right.

60
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:28,412
That is the map.

61
00:03:28,412 --> 00:03:31,837
Next lecture, we stop talking about agents and build one.
